          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have had reflux for years and now food sometimes sticks going down. How worrying is that, and what does guidance describe about when to investigate?",
          "answer": "**Please book a medical review soon, within days rather than months.** New difficulty swallowing in someone with years of reflux should be assessed, not watched. The guideline passage \"GERD: empiric initial management and the alarm features that change it\" lists difficulty swallowing as an alarm feature that calls for prompt endoscopy (a camera test of the gullet and stomach) rather than another trial of reflux treatment. The GERD summary notes that long-standing reflux can narrow the gullet over time. An automatic symptom check did not match an emergency pattern, but that is not a clearance.\n\n**Seek emergency care now (call emergency services or go to an emergency department) if you have:**\n- Being unable to swallow your own saliva, or drooling\n- Choking, or food stuck and not moving at all\n- Vomiting blood, or black, tarry stools\n- Chest pain or pressure, especially with breathlessness, sweating, nausea, or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back. The chest pain summary notes that heartburn and heart pain can feel alike, and a history of reflux does not rule out a heart cause.\n- Sudden, severe chest or back pain\n- Fever with chest pain or trouble breathing\n\n**Book a prompt appointment if:**\n- Swallowing problems are getting worse, or foods you could manage before now stick\n- You are losing weight without trying\n- You have new signs of anaemia, such as tiredness, pallor, or breathlessness on effort\n\n**Where the sources differ:** The sources agree on the direction: swallowing difficulty needs prompt assessment. They differ in how much of the guidance they could confirm. One source lists the full set of alarm features, including unintended weight loss, vomiting blood, black stools, anaemia, persistent vomiting, a lump, family history of upper gut cancer, and new symptoms at an older age. The other could only see part of that list, so it could not confirm the full set. Neither source gives an age cut-off, or says whether sticking with solids or liquids matters more. No documented disagreement about when swallowing difficulty should prompt endoscopy was found.\n\n**How strong is the evidence?** The retrieved passages do not grade the evidence behind these recommendations, so I cannot say how firm each one is.\n\n**What to tell your clinician:** Say that food is sticking and that this is new for you. Mention how long it has been happening, whether solids, liquids or both are affected, any weight loss, and how your reflux treatment has been working. Ask whether you need an endoscopy, whether the reflux could have narrowed your gullet, and whether you need blood tests. Please don't change any medicines on your own.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
